048375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049074 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049074 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049074 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049074 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049074 #edb/ref 1031 "Factoring Trinomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049074 #edb/ref 1033 89 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049074 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "d85c70bd-27ec-54bb-a459-275d176f6b8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049199 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049199 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049199 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049199 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049199 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186203942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049199 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186203943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049199 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186203944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049199 #edb/ref 1031 "Further Factoring Trinomials With Leading Coefficients" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049199 #edb/ref 1033 4951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049199 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "e2d5739b-5d8f-5b44-8b7e-0b4e50252e45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050353 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050353 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186213006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050353 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186213007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050353 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186213008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050353 #edb/ref 1031 "Adding Rational Expressions Using Polynomial Factorization" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050353 #edb/ref 1033 6150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050353 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "41f7a103-235e-5d9c-873c-7a69d7669c46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061086 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061086 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061086 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061086 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061086 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061086 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061086 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061086 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186483189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061086 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b5de56ee-fdfa-5ee6-87f8-0226508bf6e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061768 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061768 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061768 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061768 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186482982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061768 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d2c88bae-0e5d-514c-bf54-54c3a911e7e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064002 #edb/ref 1069 "e-15070" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064002 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor the quadratic expression $10a^2 + 11a + 3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064002 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44cf8c85-f228-5eaa-922d-613355cb7d5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064002 #edb/ref 1074 "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n10a^2 + 11a + 3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($10 \\times 3 = 30$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($11$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $30.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $30$ are\n\n$$\n(1,30), \\quad (2,15), \\quad (3,10), \\quad (5,6).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(5,6)$ add to $11{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5+6 & =11\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $5a + 6a,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{a}^{2}+11a+3 & = \\\\ 10{a}^{2}+5a+6a+3 & = \\\\ (10{a}^{2}+5a)+(6a+3) & = \\\\ 5a(2a+1)+3(2a+1) & = \\\\ (5a+3)(2a+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064003 #edb/ref 1069 "e-15072" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064003 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor the expression $3x^2 - 16x + 16.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064003 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ad032833-ecf8-5295-9a9f-f69f759c19b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064003 #edb/ref 1074 "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n3x^2 - 16x + 16.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($3 \\times 16 = 48$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($-16$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the product of the quadratic coefficient and constant is positive, we only need to consider the negative factor pairs of $48.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $48$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-48), \\quad (-2,-24), \\quad (-3,-16), \\quad (-4,-12), \\quad (-6,-8).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-4,-12)$ add to $-16{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)+(-12) & =-16\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-4x - 12x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-16x+16 & = \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4x-12x+16 & = \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-4x)-(12x-16) & = \\\\ x(3x-4)-4(3x-4) & = \\\\ (x-4)(3x-4) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064004 #edb/ref 1069 "e-15073" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064004 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor the quadratic expression $2c^2 + 15c - 8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064004 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "35bedd9f-bcb7-5024-bfe7-5ef6d34518bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064004 #edb/ref 1074 "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n2c^2 + 15c - 8.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times -8 = -16$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($15$).\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of $-16{:}$\n\n$$\n(1,-16),\\quad (-1,16), \\quad (2,-8), \\quad (-2,8), \\quad (4,-4)\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-1,16)$ add to $15{:}$ \n\n$$\n(-1) + 16 = 15 \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, rewrite the linear term as $- c+16c,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{c}^{2}+15c-8 & = \\\\ 2{c}^{2}-c+16c-8 & = \\\\ (2{c}^{2}-c)+(16c-8) & = \\\\ c(2c-1)+8(2c-1) & = \\\\ (2c-1)(c+8) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064247 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064247 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064247 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064247 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064247 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064247 #edb/ref 1039 "Factoring Trinomial With Positive Coefficients" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064247 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064247 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7f6a7917-f877-5263-abea-a78c0707d9fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064248 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064248 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064248 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064248 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064248 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064248 #edb/ref 1039 "Factoring Trinomials With Negative Constants" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064248 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064248 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8cfe2121-66f0-53fa-b744-041fe830bf13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064249 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064249 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064249 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064249 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064249 #edb/ref 1039 "Factoring Trinomials With Negative Coefficients and Positive Constants" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064249 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064249 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0d5115f8-05b7-5df5-92b1-b4faa95f8449" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064458 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064458 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064458 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064458 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064458 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064458 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064458 #edb/ref 1045 "Factoring Trinomials With Leading Coefficients" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064458 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186065119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064458 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064458 #edb/ref 1050 960.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064458 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "56a150ec-8529-5db2-95d7-052f09baf4bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064476 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064476 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064476 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064476 #edb/ref 1071 "$6{w}^{2}+19w+10=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064476 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "acf95ff0-c23c-5538-9064-3e039f763a54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064476 #edb/ref 1074 "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n6{w}^{2}+19w+10.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($6\\times 10=60$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($19$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $60.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $60$ are\n\n$$\n(1,60),\\,(2,30),\\,(3,10),\\,(4,15),\\,(5,12),\\,(6,10).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(4,15)$ add to $19:$ \n\n$$\n4+15=19\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $4w+15w,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{w}^{2}+19w+10 & = \\\\ 6{w}^{2}+4w+15w+10 & = \\\\ (6{w}^{2}+4w)+(15w+10) & = \\\\ 2w(3w+2)+5(3w+2) & = \\\\ (3w+2)(2w+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064616 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064616 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064616 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064616 #edb/ref 1069 "q-224920" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064616 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064616 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor the following quadratic expression.\n\n$4{x}^{2}-15x+9=(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)($\n{{field-2}}$x\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064616 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "32c14ac4-d421-5d0c-b8af-4d8855fd2e30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064616 #edb/ref 1074 "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-15x+9.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($4\\times 9=36$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($-15$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the product of the quadratic coefficient and constant is positive, we only need to consider the negative factor pairs of $36.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $36$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-36),\\,(-2,-18),\\,(-3,-12),\\,(-4,-9),\\,(-6,-6).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-3,-12)$ add to $-15:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)+(-12) & =-15\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-3x-12x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-15x+9 & = \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-3x-12x+9 & = \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-3x)-(12x-9) & = \\\\ x(4x-3)-3(4x-3) & = \\\\ (x-3)(4x-3) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065119 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065119 #edb/ref 1061 177992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065119 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c655bc80-5885-54d2-8a94-0ad9c1a41019" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065119 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065120 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065120 #edb/ref 1061 177993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065120 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "462ef775-e55e-588f-acaf-4bf8f7bdc2df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065120 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065121 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065121 #edb/ref 1061 177997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065121 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1a35042e-19b9-53df-b251-f234f0733094" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065121 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065122 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065122 #edb/ref 1061 178001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065122 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "412ba190-4d83-5be1-abb2-91fae26ca40e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065122 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065123 #edb/ref 1061 178002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065123 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e94449ef-31f1-5a5f-b3b9-9cc8fc6af17b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065123 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065449 #edb/ref 1064 "In a previous lesson, we learned how to factor quadratic trinomials whose quadratic coefficient equals one. In this lesson, we'll extend our understanding to include cases where the quadratic coefficient is a positive integer that's different from one.\n\nFor example, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}x^2+{\\color{purple}{7}}x+{\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the quadratic coefficient equals ${\\color{red}{2}}.$\n\nTo factor this trinomial, we must find two numbers that \n\n- *multiply* to give the *product* of the quadratic coefficient and the constant $({\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}} = 6),$ and\n\n- *add* to give the linear coefficient $({\\color{purple}{7}})$.\n\nWe can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:**  We start by writing down the factor pairs of $6{:}$\n\nThe factor pairs of $6$ are\n\n$$\n(1,6), \\qquad (2,3).\n$$\n\n- **Step 2:**  Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $({\\color{purple}{7}})\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+6 & =7 & ✓ \\\\ 2+3 & =5 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 3:**  Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=x+6x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+7x+3 & = \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+x+6x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 4:**  Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+x+6x+3 & = \\\\ x(2x+1)+3(2x+1) & = \\\\ (2x+1)(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! \n\nWe can check that our answer is correct by expanding the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+1)(x+3) & = \\\\ 2x\\cdot (x+3)+1\\cdot (x+3) & = \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+6x+x+3 & = \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+7x+3 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065449 #edb/ref 1065 9455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065449 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f7e6d3a2-936b-5775-9cf9-10135f0e6486" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065449 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065450 #edb/ref 1064 "To factor a quadratic expression, we must find a factor pair of the *product* of the quadratic coefficient and the constant that adds to the linear coefficient.\n\nWith that in mind, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}y^2 {\\color{purple}{\\,-\\,7}}y + {\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\nNotice that,\n\n- the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant, ${\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}} = 6$, is *positive*, while\n\n- the coefficient of the linear term $({\\color{purple}{-7}})$ is *negative*.\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the product of the quadratic coefficient and constant is positive, we need to consider the *negative* factor pairs of $6.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $6$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-6), \\quad (-2,-3).\n$$\n\nFor each pair, their *product* is positive, and their sum is *negative*, which is what we want.\n\nNow, we determine which of these factor pairs sums to the coefficient of the linear term $({\\color{purple}{-7}}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)+(-6) & =-7\\,✓ \\\\ (-2)+(-3) & =-5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-y - 6y,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-7y+3 & = \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-y-6y+3 & = \\\\ (2{y}^{2}-y)-(6y-3) & = \\\\ y(2y-1)-3(2y-1) & = \\\\ (y-3)(2y-1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065450 #edb/ref 1065 9506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065450 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5ff88dae-40df-5a63-a7bd-0d73317e94a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065450 #edb/ref 1067 "Trinomials With Negative Linear Coefficients and Positive Constants" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482821 #edb/ref 1080 "(5m+1)(m+4)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482821 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482821 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0617ff8b-9a53-5a85-9776-ca14a6b5a1e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482836 #edb/ref 1080 "(6w+1)(w+10)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482836 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #e