 1078 #edb/ref 17592186483168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483338 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f707b313-e9c4-51d8-ae46-03c5b10920ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483361 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483361 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483361 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483361 #edb/ref 1069 "q-224897" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483361 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483361 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor the following quadratic expression.\n\n$20{b}^{2}-12b+1=(2b\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)($\n{{field-2}}$b\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483361 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3b50cd1f-80a5-51f1-b3eb-2663a6e28812" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483361 #edb/ref 1074 "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n20{b}^{2}-12b+1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($20\\times 1=20$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($-12$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the product of the quadratic coefficient and constant is positive, we only need to consider the negative factor pairs of $20.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $20$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-20),\\,(-2,-10),\\,(-4,-5).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-2,-10)$ add to $-12:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)+(-10) & =-12\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-2b-10b,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{b}^{2}-12b+1 & = \\\\ 20{b}^{2}-2b-10b+1 & = \\\\ (20{b}^{2}-2b)-(10b-1) & = \\\\ 2b(10b-1)-(10b-1) & = \\\\ (2b-1)(10b-1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483365 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483365 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52254" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483365 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483365 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor $5{h}^{2}-12h-9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483365 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "433698fd-3b83-5c8a-9280-a485202f8699" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483365 #edb/ref 1074 "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n5{h}^{2}-12h-9.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($5\\times -9=-45$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($-12$).\n\nThe factor pairs of $-45$ are\n\n$$\n(1,-45),\\,\\,(-1,45),\\,\\,(3,-15),\\,\\,(-3,15),\\,\\,(5,-9),\\,\\,(-5,9).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(3,-15)$ add to $-12:$ \n\n$$\n3-15=-12\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, rewrite the linear term as $3h-15h,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{h}^{2}-12h-9 & = \\\\ 5{h}^{2}+3h-15h-9 & = \\\\ 5{h}^{2}-15h+3h-9 & = \\\\ (5{h}^{2}-15h)+(3h-9) & = \\\\ 5h(h-3)+3(h-3) & = \\\\ (5h+3)(h-3) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483415 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483415 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483415 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483415 #edb/ref 1069 "q-224935" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483415 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483415 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor the following quadratic expression.\n\n$5{b}^{2}-34b-7=(b\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)($\n{{field-2}}$b\\,+$\n{{field-3}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483415 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d54b0636-ad05-55a6-91c9-9613b500f9d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483415 #edb/ref 1074 "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n5{b}^{2}-34b-7.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($5\\times -7=-35$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($-34$).\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of $-35:$\n\n$$\n(1,-35),\\,(-1,35),\\,(5,-7),\\,(-5,7)\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(1,-35)$ add to $-34:$ \n\n$$\n1+(-35)=-34\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, rewrite the linear term as $b-35b,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{b}^{2}-34b-7 & = \\\\ 5{b}^{2}+b-35b-7 & = \\\\ (5{b}^{2}+b)-(35b+7) & = \\\\ b(5b+1)-7(5b+1) & = \\\\ (b-7)(5b+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483430 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483430 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111838" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483430 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483430 #edb/ref 1071 "$10{m}^{2}+13m+4=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483430 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f8a011fe-bff7-586f-86e9-fabb797df0e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483430 #edb/ref 1074 "We must factor the quadratic expression\n\n$$\n10{m}^{2}+13m+4.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($10\\times 4=40$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($13$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $40.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $40$ are\n\n$$\n(1,40),\\,(2,20),\\,(4,10),\\,(5,8).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(5,8)$ add to $13:$ \n\n$$\n5+8=13\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $5m+8m,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{m}^{2}+13m+4 & = \\\\ 10{m}^{2}+5m+8m+4 & = \\\\ (10{m}^{2}+5m)+(8m+4) & = \\\\ 5m(2m+1)+4(2m+1) & = \\\\ (5m+4)(2m+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
